    },
    {
      "id": "cexa-reusable-kyc-for-exchanges-and-banks-with-the-original-issuer-paid-on-every-reuse-cmd42L",
      "sector": "finance",
      "title": "CEXA: reusable KYC for exchanges and banks, with the original issuer paid on every reuse",
      "summary": "Exchanges and banks run the same identity checks on the same customers, pay an IDV provider each time, and lose over half their sign-ups at the KYC wall. The Travel Rule adds a second redundancy: institutions must identify the counterparty behind every transfer, so each joins several directories and resubmits the same license.\n\nThe Crypto Exchange Association (demo) is a runnable answer built on Verana: a trust ecosystem founded by exchanges, open to the banks that serve them. It governs one reusable credential, CEXA-Kyc, on both sides: only accredited members may issue it, and only accredited members may ask a wallet for it. Asking for KYC data is a privileged, paid act. When a member accepts another member's credential, the protocol routes the reuse fee to the institution that paid for the original check: the KYC file becomes an asset, and the customer opens an account in about a minute. A second credential, CEXA-VerifiedCounterparty, published on each member's DID and free to verify, replaces N Travel Rule directory subscriptions with one membership.\n\nThe institutions are fictional. The infrastructure is not: run the full loop today with ten integrated open-source wallets.",
      "organizationName": "Verana",
      "productionDeployment": "no",
      "status": "published",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-18T19:17:25+01:00",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-18T19:17:25+01:00",
